WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.952
At least one, not both and exactly one make different claims about a pair.

2
00:00:05.252 --> 00:00:06.767
At least one allows both.

3
00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:08.581
Not both allows neither.

4
00:00:08.881 --> 00:00:11.420
Exactly one combines the two restrictions.

5
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.216
The label is red or blue, but not both.

6
00:00:14.516 --> 00:00:16.969
The first part requires at least one color.

7
00:00:17.269 --> 00:00:19.296
The second excludes the joint case.

8
00:00:19.596 --> 00:00:24.396
So red without blue and blue without red are allowed; neither and both are not.

9
00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:27.811
The phrase or by itself would not have excluded both.

10
00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:31.503
The phrase not both by itself would not have excluded neither.

11
00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:35.451
A short answer may preserve one restriction while losing the other.

12
00:00:35.751 --> 00:00:41.063
Separate them so you can tell whether the proposed rule keeps exactly the stated cases.

13
00:00:41.363 --> 00:00:45.224
For a pair, ask whether neither is allowed and whether both are allowed.

14
00:00:45.524 --> 00:00:50.473
For otherwise, write the rule for the first case and the rule for the other case separately.

15
00:00:50.773 --> 00:00:54.251
Ask separately what the sentence requires and what it forbids.

16
00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:57.047
With neither/nor, deny each member.

17
00:00:57.347 --> 00:01:02.232
With otherwise, read both clauses before deciding whether the rule goes both ways.
